1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:23,840
I think just by having more opportunities for women at a younger age, such as a scholarship

2
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:33,520
going into university, such as encouraging more girls at primary school, secondary school level to be excited about tech.

3
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:41,840
They don't necessarily need to be sat at computer coding, but doing exercises that gets you thinking, that gets you problem solving.

4
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:52,600
Just encouraging more girls, more women to just find the joy in solving these problems and in creating things.

5
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:59,320
I think that that will go far to getting more females within the industry.

6
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:04,720
And I think at the beginning, we mentioned that being in tech does not necessarily mean you sit on the computers all day.

7
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:07,920
There are very, very many different kinds of roles.

8
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:15,840
As I've learned, there's UX UI if you're into design, you can be a product manager and help the team to develop some of the brilliant softwares.

9
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:21,640
And you can be a condensational psychologist. There's so many things that you can jump into.

10
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:33,760
I think allowing people to see that tech is such a huge field and encourage people to realise that tech needs all kinds of people because there's all kinds of roles to fill.

11
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,360
So you'll be able to find something and probably enjoy it.

12
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:43,360
Yeah, definitely. I think at a young age, I thought tech was just like the standard programming.

13
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:45,640
I'm just sitting at a computer all day long, just coding away.

14
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:54,840
But once I got into university and I did lots of different modules and spoke to more people, I realised there's so many different aspects of tech that people don't realise.

15
00:01:54,840 --> 00:02:00,840
And I think it's important to kind of raise that awareness for girls, especially when they're in secondary school.

16
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:04,560
So I recently went back to my secondary school to do a teaching module there.

17
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:09,600
And so we set up like lunchtime clubs, which are computer science based for like year sevens.

18
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:20,120
And it was just so nice to see how excited they were to be able to do something computer science related, which is actually fun and not just like, oh, write this piece of code to do this boring program.

19
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:27,600
And I think that's something that needs to be encouraged more in the school, secondary schools and primary schools, but with an emphasis on girls, especially.

20
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:38,880
So my experience was a bit different. So in kind of junior school and lower school, they were quite enthusiastic and we had the program that we did in class was quite entertaining.

21
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:44,080
Like we did a lot of scratch, which although it's quite, you know, it's block based, it's quite simplistic.

22
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:46,840
It was entertaining that a lot of people are interested in it.

23
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:54,880
There were quite a few of my GCSE class, but not many took it through to A level because I don't think this was an actual career option.

24
00:02:54,880 --> 00:03:05,080
They enjoyed it at the time, but when it came to picking more important subjects, like for you to actually study in the future beyond school, a lot of people dropped it.

25
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:11,600
So I think at that level, we also need to kind of show people because when you're younger, it's good to do exciting projects.

26
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:18,400
But when you're older, you want to see more of the kind of broader scope that technology has in a more kind of in-depth way.

27
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:23,600
Yeah. And also, it's important to talk about and not forget the history of women in tech.

28
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:28,600
Women haven't just suddenly appeared in tech spaces in the last few years.

29
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:32,120
They've always been here and have always been fighting for a place.

30
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:36,920
And as more people come, shaping a new narrative should also recognise those that came before.

31
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:42,560
And that there is a long story of women being in this place and we do belong here and always have been here.

32
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:49,320
And in fact, used to be, especially in programming, used to be the majority of programmers were women.

33
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:57,640
And I think it's really important to remember that Ada Lovelace, who is, her name is well known, but she wasn't just the first female programmer.

34
00:03:57,640 --> 00:04:00,480
She was the first programmer, the first person to do it.

35
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:04,320
So it isn't so much, I think, about changing them.

36
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:06,760
Yeah, it's not about creating a place.

37
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:13,760
So it's just about showing that there's always been a place and stepping up to take it.

